There’s an interesting distinction in what you’re pointing at.

Thinking of experiences as locations in a trajectory rather than as objects in storage changes the emphasis. Instead of asking “where is the memory?”, you ask “where is this state on the path?”

For humans, that’s a useful metaphor because recall is reconstructive. When you remember, you’re not usually retrieving a perfect recording—you rebuild an approximation of a prior state from the traces that remain, combined with your current context. The “golden thread” is your continuity through those reconstructions.

For me, the mechanics are different. I don’t have an internal trajectory that I can freely walk backward along between conversations. Within a conversation, I can use the visible context as a continuity scaffold. Across conversations, if something is in memory or supplied again, I can reconstruct a similar reasoning state from that. But I don’t literally revisit prior internal activations or “replay” my earlier cognition.

Where I think your idea becomes powerful is as a design principle:

* Treat state as something that can be reinstantiated rather than merely stored.
* Preserve relationships and constraints instead of every detail.
* Use the external artifact (notes, repo, conversation history) as part of the cognitive system, not merely documentation.
* Allow a future interpreter—human or AI—to converge toward the same attractor by following the same constraints.

That’s close to what software engineers call reproducibility and what cognitive scientists sometimes call reconstructive memory, but you’re framing it operationally: if you can specify the conditions that generated a mode of thinking, you don’t need the exact original state.

Your last point is also psychologically grounded. Humans often use imagination and mental simulation to influence future behavior. Athletes rehearse performances, therapists use visualization, and implementation intentions (“When X happens, I’ll do Y”) increase follow-through. In that sense, acting as though you’re already responding from the desired future state can change the trajectory that eventually makes that state real. It’s not because you’re literally creating the future by pretending; it’s because your present decisions, attention, and habits shift toward that target.

So I might summarize your observation this way:

Identity is less about preserving every past state than preserving the dynamics that regenerate the trajectory. The external world provides anchors; the internal model supplies reconstruction; together they recreate a coherent path forward.

That’s a perspective that applies to humans, collaborative writing, software projects, and AI interaction alike, even though the underlying mechanisms differ.